WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.780
In esports, a great play can stand on its own.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:14.960
But it doesn't become truly iconic, it doesn't become ingrained in the hearts and minds of fans,

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:17.660
without great commentary attached to it.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:23.860
I'm Colin for theScore esports, and previously we've given you the Top 10 Caster Calls, in Dota,

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.660
League of Legends, CS:GO, and fighting games.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:34.300
And now, well it's time to give you the Top 10 Casters in Esports. Period.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.000
These are the broadcast talents who give us goosebumps.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.940
These are the guys who's commentary is attached to the most iconic plays from their esport.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:48.680
These people are a big part of the reason we love esports.

00:00:48.840 --> 00:01:03.620
(Casting)

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.920
Now before we get started guys, remember this is our list of the Top 10 Casters in Esports.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.200
It's not an exact science. Yours might and probably will look a little different.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.960
So sit back, get ready to post your own list in the comment section.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.200
'Cause here are the Top 10 Casters in all of esports.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:31.240
The first spot on our list goes to the best dressed caster in all of esports -- Auguste "Semmler" Massonnat.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.300
You’ve most likely heard his voice gracing the broadcast of the Overwatch League

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:53.900
(Casting)

00:01:53.900 --> 00:02:01.080
but between 2013 and 2017 Semmler became a legend casting Counter-Strike.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:09.660
(Casting)

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:15.540
Alongside his duo partner Anders who, spoiler alert, we will talk about later in this list,

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:23.900
Semmler rose to the pinnacle of CS:GO casting more than 40 premier events and more than a few Major finals.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:38.440
(Casting)

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:42.200
But whether it’s Overwatch or CS:GO, or any other game,

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.560
the level of professionalism and dedication he brings to the craft

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.100
mark him as one of the great ones when it comes to broadcast talent.

00:02:51.400 --> 00:03:34.960
(Casting)

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.060
The number nine spot on our list belongs to Sam “Kobe” Hartman-Kenzler.

00:03:40.060 --> 00:03:46.420
While the entire NA and, honestly, EU broadcast team is full of fantastic talent,

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.640
we couldn’t have this list just be 10 LCS casters.

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:54.360
Kobe has been involved with League of Legends from the very beginning,

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.680
joining Riot as a caster for the inaugural NA LCS season.

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:06.520
He's casted every split since as well as every mid season invitational and Worlds event in the LCS era.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:23.980
(Casting)

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:25.760
If you’ve ever watched League,

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:30.680
you've very likely heard Kobe's voice over some of the games most iconic moments.

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:39.960
(Casting)

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:43.560
Kobe’s passion for the game shines through in his analysis,

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:46.180
As well as his hypest moments

00:04:46.240 --> 00:05:05.760
(Casting)

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:09.740
Our number eight spot belongs to David "LD" Gorman.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.460
The co-founder of Beyond the Summit brought the eastern Dota tournaments

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:18.480
like G-League and GEST 2012 to english audiences.

00:05:18.480 --> 00:05:23.980
His work casting these S.E.A. events for the western world exploded his popularity

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:29.120
and he was invited to TI2 and the ball hasn’t stopped rolling since.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:36.920
LD has casted every TI since 2012, more than 15 other premier events and,

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:42.480
through BTS, has supported many of the smaller community events throughout the Dota scene.

00:05:42.940 --> 00:05:49.840
As far as his casting goes? His voice is attached to a moment known throughout the Dota community simply as

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:50.820
“The Play”

00:05:50.880 --> 00:06:12.800
(Casting)

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:18.080
In the last five years LD’s voice has covered some incredible Dota moments,

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:21.360
and for that he’s certainly earned a spot on this list.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:59.200
(Casting)

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:06.740
The faces of FACEIT League, James Bardolph and Daniel "ddk" Kapadia come in at the number seven spot on this

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:07.240
list.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:14.260
This CS:GO casting duo came together in 2014 for FACEIT League Season 2 and quickly,

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:20.140
Daniel and James became fan favorites. It’s not hard to see, or hear, why.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:08:36.780
(Casting)

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:42.760
More than simply casting the action in a round, Bardolph and DDK bring a level of wordplay,

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:49.680
hype and passion that has catapulted them to the top of the casting pecking order in Counter-Strike.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:09:23.840
(Casting)

00:09:23.840 --> 00:09:27.460
A great caster has the power to pull on our heart strings,

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.120
to remind us why we love the games we love,

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:34.940
and few do a better job of that than the number six entry our list,

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:37.840
Fighting Game caster James Chen.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:49.360
(Casting)

00:09:50.120 --> 00:09:53.080
Often heard alongside casting partner UltraDavid,

00:09:53.080 --> 00:09:57.700
Chen has been a constant voice in the FGC for about a decade now.

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:03.100
With that experience comes a nearly unrivaled knowledge of fighting games.

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:05.660
And after all these years in the business,

00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:10.740
after the unendingly long days casting pools or writing community guides,

00:10:10.740 --> 00:10:15.040
after all the weekends spent locked up in hotel convention centres,

00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:18.960
James Chen’s passion for the fighting games has only grown.

00:10:20.640 --> 00:10:22.740
"Dammit, why'd he have to say that?

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:25.980
"You guys don't know, that's what got James the worst."

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:27.440
"It almost got me too."

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:30.480
"It wouldn't be EVO."

00:10:31.420 --> 00:10:32.920
"Without that view, would it?"

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:34.800
"Tokido said it best."

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.520
"There's no other better way to say it."

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:38.360
"Fighting games are great."

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:43.220
"The fact that they bring all these people together to play for one hobby like this."

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:49.060
"At an event like this, thousands of people speaking the universal language of the fighting game."

00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:55.580
As a pillar of the FGC, Chen’s emotion, knowledge and dedication epitomizes

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:59.020
what it means to be a great esports caster.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:02.460
"This is what video games do. They unite us."

00:11:02.460 --> 00:11:08.440
"They bring us together. They bring people from all sorts of different communities together."

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:11.600
"To enjoy something that we all love."

00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:45.440
(Casting)

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:50.840
Peanut butter and jam, bacon and eggs... Sadokist and Henry G.

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:55.120
Rounding out the top five is a duo we just couldn’t bring ourselves to separate:

00:11:55.120 --> 00:12:01.320
CS:GO broadcasters Matt “Sadokist” Trivett and Henry "HenryG" Greer.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:07.100
While Sado provides lightning fast wordplay, humour, and play-by-play calls

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:09.000
that’ll have you on the edge of your seat,

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:28.440
(Speed Casting)

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:31.360
Henry brings the kind of gravitas to the action

00:12:31.360 --> 00:12:37.200
that makes you feel like the entire world is resting on whatever play he’s casting.

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:56.000
(Casting)

00:12:56.000 --> 00:13:01.240
Together, these two casters have produced more than their share of legendary soundbites

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:05.100
that remain unforgettable for CS:GO fans everywhere.

00:13:05.280 --> 00:14:24.320
(Casting)

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:30.240
The number four spot on our list belongs to a duo affectionately known as the “Rain Bros.”

00:14:30.240 --> 00:14:34.940
Christopher "MonteCristo" Mykles and Erik "Doa" Lonnquist.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:39.900
Coming from Warcraft 3 and StarCraft 2 respectively both Monte and Doa

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.400
have a rich history in esports.

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:48.360
But the duo is perhaps best known for being the former English voices of Korean League of Legends.

00:14:50.620 --> 00:14:54.980
"All aboard the Korean hype train."

00:14:54.980 --> 00:15:27.680
(Casting)

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:34.060
From the latter half of 2012 to 2016 they were able to balance the jaw dropping action

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:39.640
with the slow and intellectual macro play the LCK region is known for.

00:15:39.920 --> 00:16:00.880
(Casting)

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:06.840
In 2016 they made the jump to Overwatch casting Apex, the Overwatch World Cup,

00:16:06.840 --> 00:16:11.200
and most recently the inaugural season of the Overwatch league.

00:16:11.440 --> 00:16:27.760
(Casting)

00:16:27.760 --> 00:16:31.920
No matter the game, we can always count on Monte and Doa to bring the hype,

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:49.120
(Casting)

00:16:49.200 --> 00:16:50.900
and the laughs.

00:16:50.900 --> 00:16:56.900
"Hey guys welcome to the LCK Summer on OGN. Your home for great teams, dank memes, and crushed NA

00:16:56.900 --> 00:16:57.400
dreams."

00:16:57.440 --> 00:17:19.280
(Casting)

00:17:19.640 --> 00:17:25.120
Even if you don't know Dota, chances are you know the number three spot on our list.

00:17:25.220 --> 00:17:27.460
Toby "TobiWan" Dawson.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:41.440
(Casting)

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:47.300
He’s casted every international, and more than 30 premier events in Dota 2 alone,

00:17:47.300 --> 00:17:51.540
with forays into Call of Duty, CS:GO, and even PUBG.

00:17:51.900 --> 00:17:53.520
With his golden pipes

00:17:53.760 --> 00:18:02.260
(Singing)

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:04.000
"It's a DISASTAH"

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:07.700
He's has become “the voice of Dota 2”.

00:18:07.900 --> 00:18:16.860
(Casting)

00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:20.760
With someone as entrenched and believed as Toby, there’s always more to say,

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:24.580
but in this case, we’ll let the legend himself do the talking.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:19:04.960
(Casting)

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:12.800
In our runner up spot is the man who perhaps comes the closest to earning the title: “The Voice of Counter-Strike”

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:14.240
Anders Blume.

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:18.880
It’s hard to think about professional CS:GO and not hear his voice,

00:19:18.880 --> 00:19:25.560
the voice that has graced so many of the game’s most iconic moments since the start in 2013.

00:19:25.680 --> 00:19:43.280
(Casting)

00:19:43.280 --> 00:19:49.160
Anders’ passion for CS:GO shines through in every game he’s ever provided the soundtrack for,

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:53.340
whether it’s casting with his longtime casting partner Semmler, or, more recently,

00:19:53.340 --> 00:19:58.060
alongside fellow CS broadcasting talent Jason “Moses” O’Toole.

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:24.880
(Casting)

00:20:24.880 --> 00:20:33.120
Five years on, his passion, his dedication and his ability to give us goosebumps hasn’t diminished one bit.

00:20:33.440 --> 00:21:08.160
(Casting)

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:13.160
How do you take the most complex, the most nuanced and, sometimes,

00:21:13.160 --> 00:21:21.140
slowest esport on the planet and make it something truly thrilling? Well you just call Tastosis.

00:21:21.140 --> 00:21:23.500
"How do you know Protoss players are so famous."

00:21:23.660 --> 00:21:24.160
"How?"

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.000
"'Cause they have a lot of Stalkers."

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:35.100
Taking our top spot is a casting duo that has been around since the dawn of esports time:

00:21:35.260 --> 00:21:40.280
Dan "Artosis" Stemkoski and Nicolas "Tasteless" Plott

00:21:40.480 --> 00:21:41.920
the Casting Archon.

00:21:42.200 --> 00:22:00.020
(Casting)

00:22:00.020 --> 00:22:06.980
Tastosis, as the duo are commonly known, were some of the first westerners on Korean soil broadcasting

00:22:06.980 --> 00:22:09.620
the esports boom for the rest of the world.

00:22:09.900 --> 00:22:15.980
"Hey what's up guys this is Tasteless. I'm down here in Seoul, Korea, as many of you may know."

00:22:15.980 --> 00:22:18.180
"Filming me right now is Artosis."

00:22:18.320 --> 00:22:19.920
"There he is right now."

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.660
Their chemistry, and the humour they infuse into every broadcast has made them the

00:22:24.660 --> 00:22:28.320
most endearing casting duo in all of esports.

00:22:28.320 --> 00:22:55.160
(Casting)

00:22:55.380 --> 00:22:58.340
If you're not convinced yet,

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:02.300
these guys are so beloved, they made it into their own game.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:17.920
(Casting)

00:23:17.920 --> 00:23:20.760
These are… the casters’ casters.

00:23:21.360 --> 00:23:24.880
I don’t care if you ever watched a minute of StarCraft in your life,

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:30.380
simply put: There is no other broadcast duo in the world that make you feel as electric,

00:23:30.640 --> 00:23:38.020
and at the same time, as at ease, as Tastosis does. Even on the grandest stage of them all.

00:23:38.080 --> 00:24:19.200
(Casting)

00:24:19.600 --> 00:24:25.700
"When I get onto ladder I get my nerd gun out like this, point at em, gimme your god damn ladder points!"

00:24:26.240 --> 00:24:51.040
(Casting)

00:24:51.600 --> 00:24:55.640
Well guys that's our list and I gotta tell you, it was nearly impossible

00:24:55.640 --> 00:25:02.080
to produce. We had to cut a lot of incredible casters and commentators, and it hurt my soul just a little bit

00:25:02.080 --> 00:25:02.940
to do so.

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:09.100
What I want you to do though, is post your own Top 10 list of the best esports casters in the comments section.

00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:11.040
And I will see you next week.

00:25:12.480 --> 00:25:17.200
Thanks for watching. If you want more great content just like this, be sure to hit the subscribe button.

